-- allium: 3
--
-- nixtune multiroom: several nixtune hosts in one home, each a room with
-- its own MPD, its own queue and its own volume, any of which can play
-- in sync with any other, take the music from another room, or hand its
-- music to another room. There is no controller host. Every room is a
-- peer; a group is a room whose Sendspin server is holding other rooms'
-- players, and nothing else records it.
--
-- THIS FILE AND ITS SIBLINGS. nixtune.allium covers one player: the
-- module, the hardware, the library, the renderers and their
-- arbitration. nixtune-page.allium covers the page. This file covers
-- what changes when more than one room exists: the audio transport
-- between rooms (Sendspin), the per-room daemon that serves it and
-- decides what the room's DAC plays, the credentials, the acoustic
-- alignment between rooms, interruptions, the shared library and
-- playlists, the page's rooms panel, publishing, and the failure rules.
-- Where this file changes a rule in nixtune.allium (output arbitration,
-- the database, decision 3, how the module gets its packages), it names
-- the rule.
--
-- STATUS, 2026-11-01: DRAFT, nothing built. The basis for a new branch
-- of ~/git/nixtune. Decided in two rounds of questions today:
--   transport Sendspin, server written in Haskell (M1);
--   a room alone keeps the direct path (M4);
--   nixtune-room is Haskell, a static aarch64 binary via haskell.nix
--     (M10), published from the flake with a binary cache (M17);
--   rooms declared in Nix only (M7);
--   one room writes the library database to the NAS for the others
--     (M12); saved playlists in one NAS directory (M13);
--   credentials provisioned from sops from day one, pairing mandatory,
--     with a layer or a fork where sendspin-cli lacks a way (M15);
--   AirPlay, a foreign Sendspin server and any later announcement
--     source interrupt a room and then return it to exactly where it
--     was; a group whose source room is interrupted keeps playing (M16);
--   three rooms, audible from each other, two of them wired; per-room
--     and per-pair delays are numbers declared in Nix and found with a
--     calibration step (M14).
-- Decided in a third round:
--   the rooms are kitchen, lab and living-room, all stereo (THE HOME);
--   nixtune-room for the fleet comes from the self-hosted attic cache,
--     and a cache for other adopters waits until there are some (M17);
--   the focus moves only when pinned (M14);
--   the player is sendspin-cli, patched and wrapped, with no native
--     player planned unless sendspin-cli causes a problem nixtune cannot
--     fix around it; nixtune's own Sendspin code is the server, covering
--     the spec subset nixtune uses rather than parity with the Python
--     projects (M18);
--   lab and living-room are wired, the kitchen stays on Wi-Fi, the lab
--     is the library writer, and pinning is on the rooms panel only;
--   the kitchen feeds M-Audio monitors, the lab Yamaha monitors through
--     an Ashly rack mixer, the living room KRK monitors and a sub through
--     an 8-channel Ashly rack mixer, none with DSP.
-- Build started 2026-11-01 with stage 1: nixtune-room's pure core (the
-- coordinator as a pure state machine, the audio path derived from it,
-- acoustic alignment, the spec's time filter, psk_id) and its tests.
-- Stage 2, the same day: the shell (room.json, the line protocol on a
-- websocket and the control socket, the MPD client, peer calls, the
-- clock), `nixtune-room keys` and `nixtune-room provision-player`.
-- Stage 3, the same day: the Sendspin server (M19): Noise KKpsk2 on
-- crypton, the core messages, the time responder, player@v1 with PCM,
-- send-ahead across a group, set_output_delay, stream start, clear and
-- end, and the fifo reader. Stage 4, the same day: packages/nixtune-room
-- (nixpkgs' Haskell set, open question 6), packages/sendspin (M20), the
-- module (nix/module/multiroom.nix), the AirPlay hooks through the
-- coordinator, and MultiroomRehearsal's first cut (nix/tests), all
-- evaluated but not yet built or run. Stage 5 is what M19 defers (FLAC,
-- metadata@v1, client-initiated connections, JumpClears), the
-- interrupters option, the shared library and playlists, and the
-- release order (KnownIssue release_does_not_wait_for_player).
-- The open questions at the end are the next round. One room exists:
-- the kitchen (the Pi, host nixtune).
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE HOME
-- ============================================================================
--
--   kitchen      the Pi 5 with the HiFiBerry DAC2 HD built so far, host
--                nixtune. Its room name is declared, because it differs
--                from the host name.
--                On Wi-Fi. Feeds a pair of M-Audio monitors.
--   lab          a second nixtune, to be built. Wired. Feeds an Ashly rack
--                mixer driving Yamaha studio monitors. The library
--                writer, because it is wired and rarely in use.
--   living-room  a third nixtune, to be built. Wired. Feeds an 8-channel
--                Ashly rack mixer driving two KRK studio monitors and a
--                subwoofer.
-- All three are stereo, with no DSP anywhere in the chain and none
-- planned, so every outputDelay starts at 0 and calibration confirms or
-- corrects it. Each room can be heard from the others to some degree,
-- which is why acoustics are declared for every pair. The two wired
-- rooms report the smallest buffers; the kitchen, on Wi-Fi, sets the
-- send-ahead of any group it is in.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT EXISTS, CHECKED 2026-11-01
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Synchronised playback across rooms has several mature FOSS answers,
-- and every one has the same shape: one process per group stamps each
-- chunk of audio with the time it must leave the speaker; every player
-- keeps an estimate of the offset between its clock and that process's
-- clock, measured continuously; every player corrects for its own DAC's
-- crystal running fast or slow by dropping or duplicating single
-- samples or by resampling. They differ in the clock protocol, the wire
-- format, security and who controls a group.
--
--   AES67 / Dante / RAVENNA (pro audio). PTP (IEEE 1588) with hardware
--     timestamps, a grandmaster elected by PTP's Best Master Clock
--     Algorithm, uncompressed RTP multicast at a fixed link offset.
--     Sub-microsecond. PipeWire speaks AES67 from 1.1.0 and the Pi 5's
--     Ethernet does hardware timestamping, but it needs every room
--     wired and a multicast-friendly switch. One of the three rooms
--     stays on Wi-Fi. Not used.
--   Snapcast (v0.35.0, 2026-03-10; in nixpkgs with a NixOS module).
--     Clients sync to one snapserver over the TCP connection that
--     carries the audio and correct drift by removing or duplicating
--     single samples; the README gives a typical deviation below 0.2 ms,
--     as a claim rather than a requirement. One fixed buffer per server
--     (1000 ms by default), one stream format per server, plain TCP
--     unless optional TLS is set up, and a client follows the one server
--     on its command line. Not chosen (M1).
--   Sendspin (Open Home Foundation; spec at github.com/Sendspin/spec,
--     last changed 2026-10-28). Websocket transport; a mandated
--     two-dimensional Kalman filter for clock offset and drift; a
--     normative accuracy floor of +-1 ms at the output and a target of
--     +-0.5 ms; per-player output delay that the player persists and a
--     server may set; per-player lead time from which the server sets
--     one send-ahead for the group; multiple servers by design, where a
--     player holds one admitted connection and a server that connects
--     declaring playback takes it over; external-source handling; Noise
--     KKpsk2 encryption with Curve25519 identities and pairing; roles
--     for player, controller, metadata, artwork, visualizer and colour.
--     A technical preview whose spec changed substantially in 2026.
--     Implementations: aiosendspin and Music Assistant (Python, the only
--     production servers), sendspin-cli (Python player, in nixpkgs),
--     sendspin-go (marked unmaintained 2026-10-14), sendspin-rs (Rust,
--     client only, marked work in progress). Chosen (M1).
--   AirPlay 2 (shairport-sync with NQPTP). Grouping is done by the
--     sender; NQPTP owns UDP 319 and 320. Not a group transport here.
--   Lyrion Music Server with squeezelite. One central server owns every
--     group. The model this file rejects.
--   moOde's multiroom. trx multicasting Opus over RTP with no clock sync
--     at all, a fixed delay that relies on the network delivering every
--     packet to every receiver within about 1 ms, and a sender that
--     cannot be a receiver. Nothing of it is reused.
--
-- What none of these does: let every room keep its own player and
-- queue, group and ungroup with no central server, move a session from
-- one room to another without stopping it, and align the rooms
-- acoustically for wherever the listener is. Sendspin's multiple-server
-- rule is the closest any protocol comes, and this file builds the rest
-- on it.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT TIMING NEEDS NUMBERS AND WHAT DOES NOT
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Three different delays reach a listener's ear, and only two of them
-- are declared.
--
--   Network delay, including how far a room is from the router and
--     whether it is wired. Not declared. Sendspin's clock filter measures
--     the offset between each player and the server continuously and
--     removes it, so the network's delay never shows up as a timing
--     error. What the network does change is jitter: a Wi-Fi room needs
--     a deeper buffer, it reports that as min_buffer_ms, and the server
--     sets the whole group's send-ahead from the deepest buffer any
--     member asks for. A wired room on its own, or a group of wired
--     rooms, runs with less delay.
--   Output delay, per room: anything after the DAC's output, such as an
--     amplifier or speaker with its own DSP. Declared as outputDelay.
--     Usually 0 for a passive amplifier.
--   Acoustic delay, per pair of rooms: the time sound takes from one
--     room's speakers to the place a listener stands in another room,
--     about 2.9 ms per metre. Declared as acoustics, and the only
--     number that depends on where the listener is.
--
-- A one-off "start at time T" between rooms, with latencies measured
-- once, was the first idea and was rejected: two DAC crystals 50 ppm
-- apart drift 3 ms apart every minute, and Wi-Fi delay changes packet
-- by packet. Only continuous correction at the player holds rooms
-- together, and MPD has none.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE MODEL
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Every room runs, beside MPD:
--
--   nixtune-room, one Haskell process, which is:
--     - the room's Sendspin SERVER. Its one audio source is this room's
--       MPD, through a fifo output. It connects out to the players it
--       is asked to hold, and sets each one's output delay for the
--       current focus.
--     - the room's COORDINATOR. It owns the room's intent (below), its
--       MPD output switches and its interruptions, answers pages and
--       peers, and reconciles what is with what is intended.
--     - an MPD client of its own room's MPD, for metadata, for pause and
--       skip handling, and for the output switches.
--   a Sendspin PLAYER on the room's DAC (sendspin-cli's daemon to start
--     with), always running and listening, admitting only servers it
--     holds a provisioned pairing record for, and opening the DAC only
--     after its coordinator has freed it.
--
-- Each room's coordinator is the only writer of that room's INTENT:
--
--   own        the speaker plays this room's MPD
--   following  the speaker plays another room's server; `source` names it
--   silent     the speaker plays nothing; this room's MPD may still be
--              feeding other rooms
--
-- A room's AUDIENCE is the set of other rooms' players its server holds
-- with the playback activity, plus those SUSPENDED (interrupted for a
-- while, and coming back). A room with an audience is a SOURCE. A GROUP
-- is a source plus its audience, plus the source room itself when its
-- intent is own. A player belongs to exactly one server at a time, by
-- Sendspin's own rule, so a speaker is in exactly one group and groups
-- cannot chain: a room that follows another while it has an audience is
-- a source whose own speaker is elsewhere.
--
-- An INTERRUPTION (AirPlay, a paired foreign Sendspin server, later
-- Bluetooth) sits on top of a room's intent without changing it. When it
-- ends, the room is put back exactly as it was.
--
-- What feeds the DAC follows from intent, audience and interruption:
--
--   interrupted  intent     audience  DAC fed by                    bit-perfect
--   yes          any        any       the interrupting source        -
--   no           own        none      MPD's ALSA output, as today    yes
--   no           own        some      own player, from own server    no
--   no           following  any       own player, from the source    no
--   no           silent     some      nothing; MPD feeds the server  -
--   no           silent     none      not a resting state: own, paused
--
-- ============================================================================
-- DESIGN DECISIONS
-- ============================================================================
--
--   M1. The transport is Sendspin, and nixtune's server is written in
--       Haskell inside nixtune-room. Chosen over Snapcast for the
--       specified accuracy, the per-player lead time and output delay,
--       the multiple-server rule (which is the decentralised model this
--       file needs), external-source handling, mandatory encryption, and
--       the metadata and visualizer roles the party direction will want.
--       The cost, accepted: the spec is a preview and nixtune tracks it;
--       the server is ours to write. Noise is implemented in
--       nixtune-room on crypton's X25519, ChaChaPoly1305, AES-GCM and
--       HMAC-SHA256 (M19), checked against vectors from the Python
--       noiseprotocol library that aiosendspin uses.
--   M2. No group is stored. Each room writes only its own intent; each
--       server knows only the players it holds. A group is derived by
--       asking every room for its status. Nothing is elected or
--       replicated, because no fact has two writers.
--   M3. Rooms reconcile. Each coordinator compares its intent with what
--       is (which server holds its player, whether its source still
--       exists, whether its source has moved) every
--       config.reconcile_interval and on every event, and corrects the
--       difference by asking, never by writing another room's state.
--   M4. A room alone keeps today's path exactly: MPD straight to the
--       DAC, no resampling, no format conversion. Only a room in a group
--       plays through Sendspin. Group playback is not bit-perfect: MPD
--       resamples to multiroom.streamFormat, and the player drops and
--       duplicates single samples to hold sync, which Sendspin's own
--       guidance notes leaves the audio bit-exact between corrections.
--       Switching costs a gap in the source room when its first
--       follower arrives and when its last one leaves.
--   M5. Transport commands from a room that is following go to the
--       source's MPD. A page in a following room shows the source's
--       queue and sends play, pause, next and queue edits there. One
--       queue per group; MPD stays the truth for it.
--   M6. Volume is per room, written by that room's volume helper on its
--       own hardware mixer. nixtune's server never sends a volume or
--       mute command; the only player command it sends is
--       set_output_delay (M14). The player's software volume stays at
--       100 and its hardware volume is off: sendspin-cli's
--       use_hardware_volume, on by default in daemon mode, would write
--       the DAC2 HD's mixer, which is the write nixtune.allium's
--       mpd_setvol_mutes_dac2hd says goes to mute. A group volume
--       control on the page is a relative change sent to each member
--       room's own helper.
--   M7. Rooms are declared in Nix: multiroom.peers is the same list on
--       every room, with each room's server and player public keys.
--       Servers connect to declared hosts only and never browse mDNS.
--   M8. Intent lives in /run. A room that reboots comes back own and
--       paused. A source that reboots keeps its audience, because the
--       followers did not reboot and their intent still names it; they
--       ask it to hold them again when it returns, and fall back to own
--       only after config.orphan_timeout.
--   M9. Two ways to take music to another room, the cheap one first.
--       BRING: the destination follows the source and the origin goes
--       silent; nothing is copied and nothing stops. MOVE: the
--       destination copies the source's queue and position into its own
--       MPD and becomes the source; the old source returns to own and
--       paused; its audience is taken over by the new source. MOVE has a
--       gap; it exists because a BRING leaves the old room's MPD tied up
--       feeding the group.
--   M10. nixtune-room is the first resident service in nixtune, and it
--       ends nixtune.allium's decision 3 for this feature: a server that
--       timestamps audio, answers clock exchanges and reconciles while
--       no page is open cannot be a one-shot helper. The page still
--       holds no state of its own about groups and never writes MPD
--       outputs or starts units.
--   M11. Pause and skip are immediate in a group. The source's server
--       watches its MPD; on pause, a seek, or a song change before the
--       previous song ended, it sends stream/clear so every player drops
--       what it has buffered, and on pause it seeks MPD back by the
--       audio that was sent but not yet heard, so resuming loses
--       nothing. A natural track change is not cleared, so gapless
--       albums stay gapless.
--   M12. One library database for the home. The room declared as
--       multiroom.library.writer scans, as every room does today, and
--       publishes its database file to the NAS after each scan. Every
--       other room never scans: it adopts a newer published database
--       when it is idle, by copying the file and restarting MPD. No room
--       depends on another being up to see its library.
--   M13. One saved-playlist directory for the home, on the NAS, as
--       every room's MPD playlist_directory. MPD replaces a saved
--       playlist through a temporary file and a rename, so no room ever
--       reads half a file. Two rooms editing the same playlist at the
--       same moment lose one edit (KnownIssue shared_playlist_races).
--   M14. Acoustic alignment for a FOCUS room. Rooms that can hear each
--       other line up at the listener only if each speaker leads by the
--       extra distance its sound travels to reach them. The focus is the
--       room the listener is in; for focus f, each member r of the group
--       gets output_delay = outputDelay[r] + (A - acoustics[f][r]), where
--       acoustics[f][r] is the travel time from r's speakers to f's
--       listening spot and A is the largest acoustics[f][x] over the
--       group's members. The focus room itself, nearest to the
--       listener, therefore gets the most added delay, and the farthest
--       audible room gets none. That delay is a few tens of
--       milliseconds on top of a send-ahead the whole group already
--       runs on; what the listener hears is one sound with no echo from
--       the rooms next door. Sendspin forbids negative output delays,
--       which this form never needs. A room not declared audible from f
--       is treated as if it were the focus. The focus moves only when a
--       room is pinned on the rooms panel; unpinned, it is the source
--       room when the source room is listening, otherwise the first
--       member by name. When it moves, the server sends
--       set_output_delay to every member, and the player moves to the
--       new delay gradually instead of restarting. Following the
--       listener automatically (the last page touch, presence) is
--       deferred (Multiroom.focus_follows_listener).
--   M15. Credentials are provisioned from sops, and every connection is
--       paired. Each room has a server identity and a player identity
--       (Curve25519 private keys), and every ordered pair (server room,
--       player room) has a long-term PSK. Public keys are declared in
--       Nix; private keys and PSKs live in sops. nixtune's server and
--       nixtune's players hold pairing records for each other from first
--       boot, and players run with unpaired access off. sendspin-cli has
--       no option for provisioned credentials, so nixtune provides the
--       layer (rule PlayerCredentialsProvisioned) and carries a patch
--       where sendspin-cli's behaviour is wrong for this (KnownIssue
--       player_start_hook_does_not_wait), offered upstream.
--   M16. Interruptions return the room to where it was. AirPlay starting,
--       a declared foreign Sendspin server taking the player (an
--       announcement, say), or any later renderer suspends the room's
--       part in its group for as long as it lasts and then restores the
--       room's intent, its source and its MPD's play state. A source
--       room that is interrupted keeps feeding its group; only its own
--       speaker changes. A follower that is interrupted is SUSPENDED in
--       its source's audience, so the source does not switch paths for
--       an announcement.
--   M17. nixtune-room is a package of this flake, published. haskell.nix
--       becomes an input of the public flake; packages.<sys>.nixtune-room
--       is a static musl binary for x86_64-linux and aarch64-linux
--       (cross-built), and the module defaults
--       services.nixtune.multiroom.package to this flake's own package
--       for the host's system, so a host fetches it from a binary cache
--       instead of building GHC. That changes nixosModules.default from
--       a path to a function of the flake, which nixtune.allium's
--       decision about the module bringing its own packages built with
--       the host's pkgs does not cover; this package is the exception.
--       For the fleet, the cache is the self-hosted attic: winsmuth
--       builds both systems and pushes them there, and neoblade-config
--       lists that cache as a substituter. The nixtune flake does not
--       name the attic cache in its nixConfig, because adopters would
--       then be asked to trust a private cache; a public cache for
--       adopters waits until there are adopters (KnownIssue
--       binary_cache_needed).
--   M18. The player starts as sendspin-cli, patched and wrapped, and
--       nixtune's own Sendspin code covers the spec, not the Python
--       projects. Everything in the Sendspin organisation is Apache-2.0
--       and the spec is under the Community Specification License with
--       a patent grant; both allow an AGPLv3 nixtune to implement the
--       spec, carry patches, and port logic with attribution in a
--       NOTICE file. Measured 2026-11-01 in non-blank, non-comment lines
--       of Python: aiosendspin's server 15,036, its client 3,916, its
--       Noise and pairing 3,393, its audio 1,165, and sendspin-cli
--       8,851; sendspin-rs, a client only, is 6,880 lines of Rust.
--       Parity with that is not a goal: most of it serves Music
--       Assistant, Chromecast, a party mode, terminal and web user
--       interfaces and code-based pairing for devices with screens.
--       nixtune implements the subset it uses, conformant to the spec
--       for each role it claims:
--         nixtune-room's server (needed first, there is no other non-
--           Python server): core messages, Noise KKpsk2 with
--           provisioned PSKs, the time responder, PCM (FLAC in
--           stage 5), send-ahead across a group, stream start, clear
--           and end, set_output_delay, player@v1 (metadata@v1 in
--           stage 5);
--         only if sendspin-cli causes a problem nixtune cannot fix
--           around it, a native player behind SendspinPlayer: the Noise
--           responder, the spec's time filter (an exact algorithm, about
--           a hundred lines), FLAC decoding, buffer accounting, and the
--           real-time ALSA output with sample insertion and deletion.
--           The last is the hard part, because GHC's garbage collector
--           must never stall the output; it is planned as a small C
--           ring buffer and output thread called through the FFI, with
--           Haskell doing everything else.
--       Not planned: code-based pairing (CPace), which only devices
--       without provisioned keys need; the controller, artwork,
--       visualizer and colour roles until the features that use them.
--       sendspin-cli is what plays, with nixtune's patch (KnownIssue
--       player_start_hook_does_not_wait) and nixtune's provisioning
--       layer (rule PlayerCredentialsProvisioned) around it. Neither is
--       large: the patch awaits one hook, and the layer writes two JSON
--       files from sops.
--       Build order: the server and coordinator first, developed
--       against MultiroomRehearsal (three VMs with sendspin-cli
--       players), which needs no second Pi; then the kitchen plus one
--       wired room; then calibration on the real rooms.
--   M19. Stage 3's server streams PCM, not FLAC. PCM at 44.1 kHz, 24
--       bits, stereo is 2.1 Mbit/s, which the wired rooms and the
--       kitchen's Wi-Fi carry, and it needs no C library in the static
--       build. sendspin-cli lists PCM at every rate and depth its device
--       takes, so the player runs with --audio-format pcm:<streamFormat>.
--       A player that lists PCM only at 16 bits gets the top 16 bits of
--       each sample. FLAC through libFLAC follows in stage 5, when the
--       static build is proven. Noise is written against crypton rather
--       than taken from cacophony, which depends on the deprecated
--       cryptonite; it is about 200 lines and the spec pins every
--       detail. Also deferred to stage 5: metadata@v1, the listener on
--       8927 for client-initiated connections (no nixtune player uses
--       one), and JumpClears (a seek in a group plays out the send-ahead
--       before the new position).
--   M20. The Sendspin Python packages follow upstream's release tags,
--       newer than nixpkgs, from packages/sendspin/python-overrides.nix:
--       aiosendspin 10.2.2 and sendspin-cli 9.1.0 as of 2026-11-01, the
--       releases stage 3 was tested against (aiosendspin's own suite,
--       2994 tests, and sendspin-cli's, 169 with nixtune's patch, pass
--       on them). Sendspin has no stable branch: releases are tags cut
--       from main, often several a week. A bump changes version, tag and
--       hash in that one file and is deployed only after the rehearsal
--       passes. The player is built from a Python set of its own, so a
--       host's other Python packages keep nixpkgs' versions; the flake's
--       overlays.sendspin applies the same file to every Python set for
--       a machine that wants these versions outside nixtune.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- MODULE OPTIONS (services.nixtune.multiroom.*, nix/module/multiroom.nix)
-- ============================================================================
--
--   enable          false. Off contributes nothing (nixtune.allium
--                   decision 2).
--   package         packages/nixtune-room built with the host's pkgs
--                   (open question 6 is whether this becomes M17's
--                   cached static binary).
--   playerPackage   packages/sendspin: sendspin-cli at the release M20
--                   names, with nixtune's patch.
--   room            this room's name, strMatching "[a-z][a-z0-9-]*".
--                   Defaults to networking.hostName.
--   peers           attrset by room name of { host; port; serverKey;
--                   playerKey; outputDelay; }, every room in the home,
--                   this one included, identical on every room. port is
--                   the room's page port (80), where nginx proxies /room.
--                   The keys are base64url Curve25519 public keys, which
--                   are the Sendspin server_id and client_id. outputDelay
--                   is in milliseconds, 0 to 5000.
--   acoustics       attrset of attrsets, acoustics.<focus>.<room> =
--                   milliseconds of travel from <room>'s speakers to the
--                   listening spot in <focus>. Rooms that cannot be heard
--                   from <focus> are left out. Found by calibration
--                   (rule CalibrationAdjusts) and then declared.
--   secrets         runtime paths, each to a file holding one base64url
--                   value: serverKeyFile and serverPskFiles.<playerRoom>
--                   (readable by nixtune-room), playerKeyFile and
--                   playerPskFiles.<serverRoom> (readable by
--                   nixtune-player), one PSK file per room in peers on
--                   each side. How the files get there is the consumer's
--                   business, as with the wifi passphrase; the fleet maps
--                   its sops secrets onto them.
--   playerCard      the ALSA card id the player opens; output.card by
--                   default. A rehearsal VM points it at snd-aloop's
--                   "Loopback". playerCardDevice is its PCM number, 0.
--                   The module writes the player's ~/.asoundrc with one
--                   PCM, nixtune, of type hw on that card, and runs the
--                   player with --audio-device nixtune: sendspin-cli
--                   finds devices among PortAudio's list by name prefix,
--                   and PortAudio lists the user's .asoundrc PCMs but not
--                   a bare hw:CARD=... name (found in the rehearsal run,
--                   2026-11-01).
--   timing          audienceGrace, orphanTimeout, playerReadyTimeout,
--                   maxSuspension, reconcileInterval, peerTimeout, in
--                   milliseconds, defaulting to config below.
--   interrupters    stage 5: attrset by name of { serverKey; } for
--                   foreign Sendspin servers allowed to interrupt this
--                   room's player, with their PSKs beside the peers'.
--   streamFormat    "44100:24:2". MPD resamples to it in group mode only.
--                   44.1 kHz because most of the library is 44.1 kHz and
--                   is then not resampled; 24 bits so 16-bit files pass
--                   through unchanged. Streamed as PCM (M19).
--   library.writer  stage 5: the room that scans and publishes the
--                   database.
--   library.published  stage 5: path on the NAS mount for the published
--                   database.
--   playlists.path  stage 5: path on the NAS mount for the shared
--                   playlist directory.
-- Wifi power save needs no option: the base module turns it off whenever
-- a wifi SSID is declared.
--
-- The module adds MPD's fifo output (enabled "no"), tmpfiles rules for
-- /run/nixtune-room and the fifo, the nixtune-room and
-- nixtune-player units, nginx's /room location, port 8928 in the
-- firewall, and the nixtune-room group that MPD (for the fifo), the
-- player and shairport-sync (for the control socket) are in. With
-- multiroom on, the AirPlay hooks call interruption_starts and
-- interruption_ends, and fall back to pausing MPD themselves when the
-- coordinator does not answer.
--
-- `nixtune-room keys --rooms kitchen,lab,living-room --out DIR` (run on
-- winsmuth) writes DIR/<room>.yaml for every room, mode 0600 and never
-- over an existing file, holding that room's nixtune-room/server-key,
-- nixtune-room/player-key, nixtune-room/server-psk/<player room> and
-- nixtune-room/player-psk/<server room>, ready to merge into the host's
-- sops file; and prints only the public keys, as the Nix attrset for
-- multiroom.peers, so nothing secret reaches the terminal.
--
-- capabilities.json gains a `multiroom` object: { room, peers: [{ room,
-- url }], roomPath, libraryWriter: Boolean, libraryId }, where libraryId
-- is the library.export string database.nix already stamps. MOVE
-- refuses a pair of rooms whose libraryId differs.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Given
------------------------------------------------------------

given {
    home: Home
    room: Room
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity Home {
    rooms: Set<Room>
    nas: Nas
}

-- A browser showing some room's page. It can talk to every peer.
external entity Client {
    served_by: Room
    role: host | guest
}

-- A Sendspin server that is not a nixtune room: Music Assistant, Home
-- Assistant announcements. Admitted only if declared as an interrupter
-- with a provisioned PSK; otherwise the player refuses it in the
-- handshake.
external entity ForeignServer {
    name: String
    server_key: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value Peer {
    room: String
    host: String
    server_key: String
    player_key: String
    output_delay: Duration
    link: wired | wifi
}

value StreamFormat {
    codec: flac | pcm
    rate: Integer
    bits: Integer
    channels: Integer
}

-- What the coordinator saves when an interruption starts and restores
-- when it ends.
value Resumption {
    intent: Intent
    source: Room?
    mpd_was_playing: Boolean
}

-- What a coordinator answers to `status` and pushes on every change.
value RoomStatus {
    room: String
    intent: Intent
    source: String?
    audience: Set<String>
    suspended: Set<String>
    moved_to: String?
    path: AudioPath
    interrupted_by: String?
    focus: String?
    library_id: String
    database_version: Timestamp?
}

value Group {
    source: Room
    members: Set<Room>
    focus: Room
}

-- What a MOVE copies from the old source's MPD into the new one.
value Session {
    uris: List<String>
    current: Integer?
    elapsed: Duration?
    state: playing | paused | stopped
    repeat: Boolean
    random: Boolean
    single: Boolean
    consume: Boolean
    audience: Set<Room>
    focus: Room?
    focus_pinned: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enums
------------------------------------------------------------

enum Intent { own | following | silent }

-- What feeds the DAC. `direct` is today's path.
enum AudioPath { direct | own_server | peer_server | off | interrupted }

enum Interrupter { airplay | upnp | foreign_sendspin }

enum Outcome { done | refused }

enum RefusalReason {
    unknown_room
    self
    library_differs
    source_unreachable
    source_is_following
    player_not_ready
    interrupted
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The room's Sendspin server, inside nixtune-room.
contract SendspinServer {
    hold: (player: Peer) -> Outcome
    suspend: (player: Peer) -> Outcome
    release: (player: Peer) -> Outcome
    audience: () -> Set<Peer>
    clear: () -> Result
    set_delay: (player: Peer, delay: Duration) -> Result

    @invariant ConformsToSpec
        -- Core messages version 1; role player@v1. metadata@v1 is
        -- stage 5; controller@v1, artwork@v1 and visualizer@v1 are
        -- deferred. Both cipher suites. server/time answered per spec,
        -- stamped immediately before encryption, from CLOCK_MONOTONIC in
        -- microseconds. The server logs the spec commit it implements
        -- at start; server/hello carries only the fields the spec
        -- defines, because senders must not add others.

    @invariant OneSource
        -- The only audio is this room's MPD, read from its fifo output
        -- in multiroom.streamFormat, except the click track of a
        -- calibration (rule CalibrationAdjusts), which the server
        -- generates itself. The server never decodes files.

    @invariant ServerInitiatedOnly
        -- The server connects out to declared players' hosts on port
        -- 8928, path /sendspin, and never browses mDNS. The listener on
        -- 8927 for client-initiated connections, which servers must
        -- support, is stage 5 (M19): no nixtune player initiates.

    @invariant PairedOnly
        -- Every handshake uses a provisioned long-term PSK. A client_id
        -- that is not a declared player key is refused in the
        -- handshake. No Sentinel-keyed session ever reaches playback.

    @invariant OneSendAheadForTheGroup
        -- The send-ahead is the maximum over held players of
        -- min_buffer_ms plus output_delay_ms, recomputed when a player
        -- joins, leaves or reports new timing. MPD's fifo output paces
        -- in real time, so the stream is live in the spec's sense.

    @invariant OnlyDelayCommands
        -- M6. server/command is only ever set_output_delay.

    @invariant OwnPlayerIsNoMember
        -- The room's own player, held for own_server, is never reported
        -- to the coordinator as joining or leaving the audience: the
        -- coordinator decides that path itself, and a report would make
        -- it release the player.

    @guidance
        -- Haskell: websockets for the transport, Noise on crypton (M19),
        -- stm for the shared state, and the MPD protocol spoken directly
        -- over the loopback socket. Chunks of 20 ms; the spec bounds them
        -- at 15 to 150 ms. MPD's output: type "fifo", name "multiroom",
        -- path /run/nixtune-room/fifo, format <streamFormat>, disabled
        -- at boot. MPD sets up its fifo output when it starts, enabled
        -- or not, and fails when the fifo's directory is missing; it
        -- starts before nixtune-room, so systemd-tmpfiles makes
        -- /run/nixtune-room and the fifo at boot (found in the first
        -- rehearsal run, 2026-11-01). A fifo MPD finds already there, it
        -- never removes. nixtune-room holds it open read-write, so MPD
        -- never finds it without a reader, and makes it only if it is
        -- missing. MPD's 24-bit format is 24 bits in
        -- 32-bit containers; the server packs it to 3 bytes for PCM.
        -- Each player has a sending thread with two queues, control
        -- before audio, so server/command and group/update go ahead of
        -- audio not yet started, as the spec requires. A pause clears
        -- every stream, then discards what the fifo still delivers until
        -- it has been quiet for 50 ms, and seeks MPD back by that audio
        -- plus what was sent and not yet heard. suspend keeps a player in
        -- the audience for path decisions while another server holds it.
}

-- The room's Sendspin player on the DAC.
contract SendspinPlayer {
    client_id: () -> String

    @invariant AlwaysListening
        -- Runs whenever multiroom is enabled, on port 8928, so a peer
        -- can hold it and an interrupter can reach it at any time.

    @invariant PairedServersOnly
        -- Unpaired access off. It admits only servers for which it
        -- holds a provisioned pairing record: the declared rooms'
        -- servers and the declared interrupters.

    @invariant OpensDacOnlyWhenFreed
        -- On stream/start it runs its start hook, which asks the room's
        -- coordinator to free the DAC, and opens the output only after
        -- the hook returns. On stream/end it closes the output and runs
        -- its stop hook. Both hooks carry the server_id, which is how the
        -- coordinator tells a peer from an interrupter.

    @invariant NoVolumeOfItsOwn
        -- Software volume fixed at 100; hardware volume off.

    @guidance
        -- To start with: sendspin-cli's `sendspin daemon` at the release
        -- M20 names (packages/sendspin), with
        -- --name <room>, --audio-device <output.device>, --port 8928,
        -- --audio-format pcm:<streamFormat>, --hardware-volume false,
        -- --unpaired-access false, --output-delay-ms <outputDelay>,
        -- --hook-start and --hook-stop calling the coordinator. Its
        -- settings, identity and pairing store live in its
        -- StateDirectory and are written by PlayerCredentialsProvisioned
        -- before every start. A native player replaces it later behind
        -- this contract; that is the hardest part of the design
        -- (real-time ALSA output and drift correction) and is
        -- deliberately not first.
}

-- The room's authority, inside nixtune-room.
contract Coordinator {
    status: () -> RoomStatus
    follow: (target: String) -> Outcome
    own: () -> Outcome
    silence: () -> Outcome
    hold: (room: String) -> Outcome
    suspend: (room: String) -> Outcome
    move_from: (source: String) -> Outcome
    hand_over: (to: String) -> Session
    focus: (room: String, pinned: Boolean) -> Outcome
    interruption_starts: (by: Interrupter, server_key: String?) -> Outcome
    interruption_ends: (by: Interrupter) -> Outcome
    calibrate: (focus: String, room: String, travel: Duration) -> Outcome

    @invariant SingleWriter
        -- Only room r's coordinator writes r.intent, r.source,
        -- r.moved_to, r.resumption, r's MPD output switches and r's
        -- interruption state. Requests from pages and peers are requests.

    @invariant Serialised
        -- One request at a time per coordinator, in arrival order.

    @invariant AnswersFromMemory
        -- status never waits on a peer, so a page drawing every room is
        -- never held up by one room that is down.

    @invariant HooksAnswerFast
        -- interruption_starts returns as soon as the DAC is free, so
        -- an AirPlay or Sendspin stream is never held up by a peer call.

    @guidance
        -- A line protocol, one request per line, one reply per line, plus
        -- pushed status lines (nixtune-room's Nixtune.Room.Wire):
        --   follow <room> | own | silence | hold <room> | release <room>
        --   | suspend <room> | pin <room> | unpin
        --   | interruption-starts <by> | interruption-ends <by>
        --                     -> ok | refused <reason>
        --   status            -> status room=.. intent=.. source=..
        --                        audience=a,b suspended=.. moved-to=..
        --                        path=.. interrupted-by=.. focus=..
        --   subscribe         -> a status line now, then one per change
        -- with "-" for an empty value and <by> one of airplay, upnp,
        -- foreign-sendspin. Served by nixtune-room itself as a websocket
        -- on 127.0.0.1:8605 behind nginx /room, the same reach as the
        -- other helpers; peers use the same endpoint through the peer's
        -- nginx. The AirPlay hooks and the player's hooks use the Unix
        -- control socket /run/nixtune-room/control.sock instead, through
        -- `nixtune-room ctl <request>`, which exits 0 for ok and 1 for a
        -- refusal, so a hook never depends on nginx.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Room {
    name: String
    host: String
    peers: Set<Peer>
    intent: Intent
    source: Room?
    moved_to: Room?
    audience: Set<Room>
    suspended: Set<Room>
    held_by: Room?
    path: AudioPath
    interrupted_by: Interrupter?
    resumption: Resumption?
    focus: Room?
    focus_pinned: Boolean
    mpd_playing: Boolean
    alsa_output_enabled: Boolean
    fifo_output_enabled: Boolean
    output_delay: Duration
    library_id: String
    library_writer: Boolean
    library_published: String
    database_version: Timestamp?
    source_unreachable_since: Timestamp?
    audience_empty_since: Timestamp?

    invariant SourceOnlyWhenFollowing {
        (intent = following) = (source != null)
    }

    invariant NeverFollowsItself {
        source != this
    }

    invariant InterruptedHasResumption {
        (path = interrupted) = (interrupted_by != null)
        and (interrupted_by != null) = (resumption != null)
    }

    invariant AlsaOutputOnlyWhenDirect {
        alsa_output_enabled implies path = direct
    }

    invariant FifoOutputOnlyWithListeners {
        fifo_output_enabled implies
            ((audience + suspended).count > 0 or audience_empty_since != null or path = own_server)
    }

    invariant HeldByMatchesPath {
        (path = own_server implies held_by = this)
        and (path = peer_server implies held_by = source)
    }

    invariant FocusIsAMember {
        focus != null implies focus in DerivedGroup(this).members
    }
}

entity HandoffRun {
    kind: bring | move
    origin: Room
    destination: Room
    source: Room
    started: Timestamp
    finished: Timestamp?
    gap: Duration?
    outcome: Outcome?
}

entity CalibrationRun {
    focus: Room
    room: Room
    travel: Duration
    started: Timestamp
    finished: Timestamp?
}

entity PublishedDatabase {
    writer: Room
    path: String
    version: Timestamp
    library_id: String
}

entity KnownIssue {
    name: String
    status: open | closed
    effect: String
    closes_when: String
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    room_port: Integer = 8605
    server_port: Integer = 8927
    player_port: Integer = 8928
    stream_format: StreamFormat = { codec: pcm, rate: 44100, bits: 24, channels: 2 }
    reconcile_interval: Duration = 2.seconds
    audience_grace: Duration = 10.seconds
    orphan_timeout: Duration = 3.minutes
    player_ready_timeout: Duration = 10.seconds
    max_suspension: Duration = 30.minutes
    move_gap_budget: Duration = 2.seconds
    database_poll: Duration = 5.minutes
    max_output_delay: Duration = 5.seconds
}

-- orphan_timeout is longer than a Pi 5 takes to reboot, so a source that
-- reboots keeps its audience (M8). audience_grace keeps a source on its
-- server path through a follower's reconnect, so a Wi-Fi blip in one
-- room does not cost the source room two gaps. player_ready_timeout
-- covers the player's start plus its time filter converging, before
-- which a Sendspin player must not report itself available.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

default KnownIssue spec_is_preview = {
    name: "Sendspin is a technical preview",
    status: open,
    effect: "a spec change can break nixtune's server against newer players, or the pinned sendspin-cli against nixtune's server",
    closes_when: "Sendspin declares a stable core and player@v1; until then the server names the spec commit it implements and the rehearsal pins the player version"
}

default KnownIssue player_store_is_private_format = {
    name: "credentials reach sendspin-cli through its own state files",
    status: open,
    effect: "PlayerCredentialsProvisioned writes identity-daemon.json and pairing-store-daemon.json in the formats aiosendspin's FileClientPairingStore and sendspin-cli's settings use today; those are implementation files, not a documented interface, and a new version can change them",
    closes_when: "sendspin-cli takes provisioned credentials through a documented option (offered upstream), or the native player replaces it; until then the rehearsal fails on any format change because the player then refuses every server"
}

default KnownIssue player_start_hook_does_not_wait = {
    name: "sendspin-cli runs its start hook without waiting for it",
    status: open,
    effect: "daemon.py starts hook_start as a background task (create_task), so the player can open the DAC while MPD still holds it; the open fails and the interruption or the group join has no sound in that room",
    closes_when: "upstream sendspin-cli waits for hook_start before opening the output, or offers an option for it; until then nixtune carries packages/sendspin/hook-start-waits.patch (2026-11-01), which drops audio chunks until the hook returns, since the device opens on the first chunk; sendspin-cli's suite passes with it, plus a test that fails without it"
}

default KnownIssue release_does_not_wait_for_player = {
    name: "a room re-enables MPD's ALSA output before its player has closed the DAC",
    status: open,
    effect: "rule OwnRequested says to wait for the player's stop hook before enabling the ALSA output; stage 2's coordinator enables it as soon as the source answers the release, while the player closes the device in its own thread after stream/end arrives. If MPD opens the card in between, the open fails busy and MPD retries the output only after about ten seconds",
    closes_when: "the player's stop hook reports the release to the coordinator (a new player-released request on the control socket) and the plan enables the ALSA output only after it, with a timeout; the rehearsal on snd-aloop and the real rooms show no busy opens"
}

default KnownIssue player_does_not_report_commanded_delay = {
    name: "aiosendspin applies set_output_delay without reporting it",
    status: open,
    effect: "the spec requires a client/state whenever output_delay_ms changes, including through server/command; aiosendspin 0.0.0 (the library sendspin-cli is built on) changes the delay and sends nothing, so a server that trusts reports alone keeps the old send-ahead and the player's buffer shrinks by the delay. nixtune's server counts a delay as applied once it queues the command (found in stage 3's end-to-end run against aiosendspin's client)",
    closes_when: "aiosendspin sends client/state after applying a delay command (offered upstream); the server's handling stays correct either way"
}

default KnownIssue player_starts_only_with_dac_free = {
    name: "the player cannot start while MPD holds the DAC",
    status: open,
    effect: "PortAudio opens each device while listing them and leaves out one it cannot open, so a player that starts while MPD plays straight to the DAC does not find its device and exits. At boot MPD starts paused, so the player starts; a player restarted during playback retries every two seconds (no start limit) until MPD pauses or the room groups. When the room groups, the source's server keeps trying to connect to it for up to the player-ready timeout",
    closes_when: "sendspin-cli opens its configured ALSA device without needing it listed, or the native player; the rehearsal cannot show it because its MPD plays to a null output"
}

default KnownIssue shared_playlist_races = {
    name: "two rooms editing one saved playlist at once",
    status: open,
    effect: "the later save wins and the earlier edit is lost; MPD's stored_playlist idle event fires only for changes made through that MPD, so a page sees another room's edit only when it lists playlists again",
    closes_when: "accepted for a household; revisit only if it happens"
}

default KnownIssue nas_permissions_for_mpd = {
    name: "every room's mpd user must write the shared playlist directory and the writer room the published database",
    status: open,
    effect: "the Synology checks the client uid against the folder's ACLs (the _youtube lesson in nixtune.allium); without the same mpd uid on every room and an ACL for it, saves fail with permission denied",
    closes_when: "users.users.mpd.uid is pinned by the module when multiroom is enabled and the README's NAS section names the ACL"
}

default KnownIssue renderer_hooks_pause_group = {
    name: "the AirPlay hooks pause MPD unconditionally",
    status: open,
    effect: "an AirPlay stream starting in a source room would pause the whole group",
    closes_when: "the hooks call interruption_starts and interruption_ends when multiroom is enabled (done in stage 4's airplay.nix), and the rehearsal covers it (not yet: the rehearsal VMs have no AirPlay sender)"
}

default KnownIssue binary_cache_needed = {
    name: "adopters need a binary cache for nixtune-room",
    status: open,
    effect: "without one, an adopter's first build of a multiroom host builds GHC and the cross toolchain, which takes hours and is the opposite of 'anyone who can flash a card'",
    closes_when: "a public cache exists for adopters, the flake names it in nixConfig, and CI pushes both systems' nixtune-room to it; the fleet already pulls from the self-hosted attic (M17)"
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: boot and credentials
------------------------------------------------------------

rule RoomBoots {
    when: HostBootsGeneration(room)
    ensures:
        room.intent = own
        room.source = null
        room.moved_to = null
        room.audience = {}
        room.suspended = {}
        room.held_by = null
        room.path = direct
        room.interrupted_by = null
        room.resumption = null
        room.focus = null
        room.focus_pinned = false
        room.alsa_output_enabled = true
        room.fifo_output_enabled = false

    @guidance
        -- MPD's state file remembers enabled outputs across restarts.
        -- nixtune-room does not trust it: before it accepts its first
        -- request it sends enableoutput for the ALSA output and
        -- disableoutput for multiroom.
}

rule PlayerCredentialsProvisioned {
    when: PlayerUnitStarts(room)
    ensures:
        PlayerIdentity(room) = Secret(room.secrets.playerPrivateKey)
        PlayerPairingRecords(room) =
            { for p in room.peers: Record(server_key: p.server_key, psk: Secret(room.secrets.psks[p.room][room.name])) }
            + { for i in room.interrupters: Record(server_key: i.server_key, psk: Secret(room.secrets.psks[i.name][room.name])) }
        PlayerUnpairedAccess(room) = false

    @guidance
        -- The player unit's ExecStartPre, `nixtune-room provision-player
        -- --state-dir <the player's StateDirectory>`, reads the
        -- sops-decrypted files and writes identity-daemon.json
        -- ({"private_key": <base64url>}) and pairing-store-daemon.json
        -- (records keyed by psk_id = base64url(SHA-256(
        -- "sendspin-psk-id-v1" || PSK)), each with psk_id, psk,
        -- server_id, used, created_at and last_used_at; pairing_config
        -- with every pairing method and unpaired access off), mode 0600,
        -- replacing whatever was there. The declaration is the whole
        -- truth: a record not declared does not survive a restart. The
        -- one value kept is last_playback_server_id, which the player
        -- uses to choose between servers, and only if it names a
        -- declared server. It refuses to write anything if the public
        -- key derived from the private key is not peers.<room>.playerKey.
        -- Checked 2026-11-01 by loading the written files with
        -- aiosendspin's own FileClientPairingStore and Identity.
}

rule ServerCredentialsLoaded {
    when: RoomDaemonStarts(room)
    ensures:
        ServerIdentity(room) = Secret(room.secrets.serverPrivateKey)
        ServerPairingRecords(room) =
            { for p in room.peers: Record(client_key: p.player_key, psk: Secret(room.secrets.psks[room.name][p.room])) }

    @guidance
        -- nixtune-room refuses to start if its derived public key is not
        -- peers.<room>.serverKey, or if any declared peer lacks a PSK.
        -- The server_id must never change (identity in sops, not
        -- generated), or every player sees a different server.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: requests a room makes about itself
------------------------------------------------------------

rule FollowRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(room, follow, target)
    let t = Room{name: target}
    ensures:
        if target not in room.peers:
            Reply(refused, unknown_room)
        else if t = room:
            Reply(refused, self)
        else if room.path = interrupted:
            Reply(refused, interrupted)
        else if not Reachable(t):
            Reply(refused, source_unreachable)
        else:
            if room.mpd_playing and (room.audience + room.suspended).count = 0:
                room.mpd_playing = false
            room.alsa_output_enabled = false
            room.intent = following
            room.source = t
            room.path = peer_server
            CoordinatorReceives(t, hold, room.name)
            Reply(done)
            RoomStatusChanged(room: room)

    @guidance
        -- Order: pause this room's MPD if it was playing alone, disable
        -- its ALSA output so the DAC is free, then ask t to hold the
        -- player. The reply waits for t's answer. If t refuses, undo in
        -- reverse and pass the reason on; MPD stays paused where it was.
        -- A second follow while one is waiting is refused busy; own or
        -- silence while one is waiting answers the waiting one busy.
        -- A room with an audience may follow: its MPD keeps feeding its
        -- own server and only its speaker moves. If t is itself
        -- following, t still holds this player; what t's server streams
        -- is t's MPD, so there is no chain.
}

rule OwnRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(room, own)
    requires: room.path != interrupted
    ensures:
        room.intent = own
        room.source = null
        if (room.audience + room.suspended).count > 0:
            SendspinServerHolds(room, room)
            room.held_by = room
            room.path = own_server
        else:
            ReleaseOwnPlayer(room)
            room.held_by = null
            room.alsa_output_enabled = true
            room.path = direct
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)

    @guidance
        -- With an audience, the room's own server holds its own player,
        -- which by Sendspin's rule displaces the source that held it;
        -- that source sees client/goodbye another_server. Without one,
        -- the coordinator asks the source to release the player
        -- (ReleaseOwnPlayer), waits for the player's stop hook, then
        -- enables the ALSA output. The room returns to its own queue,
        -- paused where it was.
}

rule SilenceRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(room, silence)
    requires: room.path != interrupted
    ensures:
        if (room.audience + room.suspended).count > 0:
            room.intent = silent
            room.source = null
            ReleaseOwnPlayer(room)
            room.held_by = null
            room.alsa_output_enabled = false
            room.path = off
        else:
            room.mpd_playing = false
            room.intent = own
            room.source = null
            ReleaseOwnPlayer(room)
            room.held_by = null
            room.alsa_output_enabled = true
            room.path = direct
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: a source's server
------------------------------------------------------------

rule HoldRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(source, hold, follower)
    let f = Room{name: follower}
    ensures:
        if follower not in source.peers:
            Reply(refused, unknown_room)
        else:
            if source.path = direct:
                SwitchToOwnServer(source)
            source.fifo_output_enabled = true
            source.suspended = source.suspended - f
            SendspinServerHolds(source, f)
            ApplyFocus(source)
            Reply(done)

    @guidance
        -- The server connects to f's host:8928/sendspin, completes the
        -- Noise handshake with the pair's provisioned PSK, then sends
        -- server/activate with activities ['playback'] and player@v1
        -- (metadata@v1 in stage 5). The reply waits for the player's first
        -- client/state with available true, up to
        -- config.player_ready_timeout; after that it is refused
        -- player_not_ready and the server disconnects.
        -- Until the player accepts the connection, the server tries again
        -- every half second while the hold stands: the player may be
        -- between restarts (player_starts_only_with_dac_free) when the
        -- follower has just freed its DAC. Each try goes through every
        -- address the host's name resolves to, in order, because a name
        -- can resolve to IPv6 first while the player and nginx listen on
        -- IPv4 only; peer calls connect the same way.
}

-- The order is fixed: MPD cannot release the DAC while its ALSA output
-- is enabled and playing, and the player cannot open it until it has.
rule SwitchToOwnServer {
    when: SwitchToOwnServer(room)
    requires: room.intent = own
    requires: room.path = direct
    ensures:
        room.fifo_output_enabled = true
        room.alsa_output_enabled = false
        SendspinServerHolds(room, room)
        room.held_by = room
        room.path = own_server

    @guidance
        -- enableoutput multiroom; disableoutput <alsa>; the server holds
        -- its own player like any other. The source room hears a gap of
        -- about one send-ahead plus the player's start. The audience
        -- does not: it joins a stream already running.
}

rule AudienceJoins {
    when: PlayerAvailable(source, follower)
    ensures:
        source.audience = source.audience + follower
        source.audience_empty_since = null
        RoomStatusChanged(room: source)
}

rule AudienceSuspends {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(source, suspend, follower)
    ensures:
        source.audience = source.audience - follower
        source.suspended = source.suspended + follower
        ApplyFocus(source)
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: source)

    @guidance
        -- Sent by a follower's coordinator when an interruption starts in
        -- it (M16). The source keeps its path and its stream; when the
        -- interruption ends, the follower asks to be held again. A
        -- suspended member that has not come back within
        -- config.max_suspension is dropped, because a follower that is
        -- interrupted and then reboots never says so.
}

rule AudienceLeaves {
    when: PlayerGone(source, follower)
    requires: follower not in source.suspended
    ensures:
        source.audience = source.audience - follower
        if (source.audience + source.suspended).count = 0:
            source.audience_empty_since = now
        ApplyFocus(source)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: source)

    @guidance
        -- PlayerGone is a closed connection, client/goodbye for any
        -- reason, client/state with available false, or client/leave.
}

rule AudienceGoneForGood {
    when: source: Room.audience_empty_since + config.audience_grace <= now
    requires: (source.audience + source.suspended).count = 0
    ensures:
        source.audience_empty_since = null
        source.fifo_output_enabled = false
        source.focus = null
        source.focus_pinned = false
        if source.intent = own and source.path = own_server:
            ReleaseOwnPlayer(source)
            source.held_by = null
            source.alsa_output_enabled = true
            source.path = direct
        if source.intent = silent:
            source.mpd_playing = false
            source.intent = own
            source.alsa_output_enabled = true
            source.path = direct
        if source.intent = following:
            -- Its speaker is elsewhere and nobody hears its MPD.
            source.mpd_playing = false
        RoomStatusChanged(room: source)

    @guidance
        -- Pause before switching outputs, so a silent room does not
        -- start playing into its own speaker. Then release the player,
        -- disableoutput multiroom, enableoutput <alsa>.
}

-- M11.
rule PauseClears {
    when: MpdStateChanges(source, playing, paused)
    requires: source.fifo_output_enabled
    let unheard = LastChunkEnd(source) - ServerNow(source)
    ensures:
        SendspinServerClears(source)
        MpdReceives(source, seekcur(-unheard))
}

rule JumpClears {
    when: MpdSongOrPositionJumps(source)
    requires: source.fifo_output_enabled
    ensures: SendspinServerClears(source)

    @guidance
        -- A jump is a seek, or a song change that came before the
        -- previous song's elapsed time reached its duration minus one
        -- chunk. A song change at the end of a song is a natural track
        -- change and is not cleared. This tells the cases apart for every
        -- MPD client (pages, Home Assistant, mpc), because they all go
        -- through MPD rather than through nixtune-room.
}

rule MetadataFollowsMpd {
    when: MpdCurrentSongChanges(source)
    requires: source.fifo_output_enabled
    ensures: SendspinServerSendsMetadata(source, CurrentSong(source))
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: acoustic alignment (M14)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ApplyFocus {
    when: ApplyFocus(source)
    let members = DerivedGroup(source).members - source.suspended
    let f = if source.focus_pinned and source.focus in members then source.focus else SourceListeningRoom(source)
    let travel = (r) => if source.acoustics[f][r] != null then source.acoustics[f][r] else source.acoustics[f][f] ?? 0
    let a = max(for r in members: travel(r))
    ensures:
        source.focus = f
        for r in members:
            SendspinServerSetsDelay(source, r, r.output_delay + (a - travel(r)))

    @guidance
        -- SourceListeningRoom is the source room when its intent is own,
        -- otherwise the first follower by name. Each delay is clamped to
        -- 0 to config.max_output_delay. A player that does not list
        -- set_output_delay in supported_commands keeps its declared
        -- outputDelay and the status shows it as unaligned.
}

rule FocusRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(source, focus, room, pinned)
    ensures:
        source.focus = Room{name: room}
        source.focus_pinned = pinned
        ApplyFocus(source)
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: source)
}

-- Calibration, by ear first, in the spirit of AirPlay's sync test: the
-- listener stands in the focus room, the source plays a click track to
-- the focus room and ONE other room, and the listener moves a slider
-- until the two clicks merge into one. The slider changes that room's
-- travel time live. When it sounds right, the page shows the number to
-- declare as acoustics.<focus>.<room>. Nothing is saved on the Pi.
rule CalibrationAdjusts {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(source, calibrate, focus, room, travel)
    ensures:
        CalibrationRun.created(focus: focus, room: room, travel: travel, started: now)
        SourcePlaysClicks(source)
        TemporaryAcoustics(source, focus, room) = travel
        ApplyFocus(source)
        Reply(done)

    @guidance
        -- The click track is a 1 kHz tone burst of 5 ms every 750 ms,
        -- generated by the server in place of MPD's audio, with MPD
        -- paused for the run. Rooms other than the two are held but
        -- silent. Leaving calibration drops every temporary value and
        -- reapplies the declared ones, so the declaration stays the only
        -- source of these numbers. Wired rooms first: their buffers are
        -- smallest and they settle fastest.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: reconciliation (M3)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule FollowerReconciles {
    when: every config.reconcile_interval for room: Room where intent = following and path != interrupted
    let s = room.source
    ensures:
        if not Reachable(s):
            if room.source_unreachable_since = null:
                room.source_unreachable_since = now
        else:
            room.source_unreachable_since = null
            if s.status.moved_to != null:
                room.source = Room{name: s.status.moved_to}
                CoordinatorReceives(room.source, hold, room.name)
            else if room.name not in s.status.audience:
                -- The source rebooted, or dropped the player.
                CoordinatorReceives(s, hold, room.name)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)
}

rule ListenerOrphaned {
    when: room: Room.source_unreachable_since + config.orphan_timeout <= now
    requires: room.intent = following
    ensures:
        room.source_unreachable_since = null
        room.intent = own
        room.source = null
        room.mpd_playing = false
        if (room.audience + room.suspended).count > 0:
            SendspinServerHolds(room, room)
            room.held_by = room
            room.path = own_server
        else:
            room.held_by = null
            room.alsa_output_enabled = true
            room.path = direct
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)

    @guidance
        -- Reachable means the source's coordinator answers status. The
        -- room returns to its own queue, paused.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: interruptions (M16; changes nixtune.allium's arbitration)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- nixtune.allium's AirplayAcquiresOutput and AirplayReleasesOutput hold
-- unchanged when multiroom is off. When it is on, shairport-sync's
-- before-play and after-play hooks call interruption_starts(airplay)
-- and interruption_ends(airplay), and the player's start and stop hooks
-- call them with foreign_sendspin when the server_id they carry is a
-- declared interrupter. A start hook carrying a peer room's server_id
-- is a group join, not an interruption.

rule InterruptionStarts {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(room, interruption_starts, by, server_key)
    requires: room.path != interrupted
    ensures:
        room.resumption = Resumption{
            intent: room.intent,
            source: room.source,
            mpd_was_playing: room.mpd_playing and (room.audience + room.suspended).count = 0
        }
        if room.intent = following:
            CoordinatorReceives(room.source, suspend, room.name)
            if by != foreign_sendspin:
                ReleaseOwnPlayerAt(room.source, room)
        if room.intent = own and (room.audience + room.suspended).count > 0:
            -- The group keeps playing; only this room's speaker changes.
            if by != foreign_sendspin:
                ReleaseOwnPlayer(room)
        if room.intent = own and (room.audience + room.suspended).count = 0:
            if room.mpd_playing:
                room.mpd_playing = false
            room.alsa_output_enabled = false
        room.held_by = null
        room.interrupted_by = by
        room.path = interrupted
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)

    @guidance
        -- For a foreign Sendspin server, the player has already been
        -- taken by the interrupter (Sendspin's multiple-server rule), so
        -- the coordinator only records it and frees the DAC from MPD if
        -- MPD had it. For AirPlay, the coordinator releases the player
        -- so shairport-sync can open the card. Reply as soon as the DAC
        -- is free (HooksAnswerFast); the suspend to the source is sent
        -- after the reply.
}

rule InterruptionEnds {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(room, interruption_ends, by)
    requires: room.interrupted_by = by
    let r = room.resumption
    ensures:
        room.interrupted_by = null
        room.resumption = null
        room.intent = r.intent
        room.source = r.source
        if r.intent = following:
            room.path = peer_server
            CoordinatorReceives(r.source, hold, room.name)
        else if (room.audience + room.suspended).count > 0:
            SendspinServerHolds(room, room)
            room.held_by = room
            room.path = if r.intent = own then own_server else off
        else:
            room.alsa_output_enabled = true
            room.path = direct
            if r.mpd_was_playing:
                room.mpd_playing = true
        Reply(done)
        RoomStatusChanged(room: room)

    @guidance
        -- The room is put back exactly as it was: following the same
        -- source, or feeding the same audience, or playing alone where
        -- it paused. If the source it followed went away in the meantime,
        -- reconciliation and orphan_timeout take it from there. This
        -- deliberately goes further than Sendspin's own rule for a
        -- player that becomes unavailable (move it to a solo group, do
        -- not rejoin it automatically): the rejoin is an explicit hold
        -- request from the room's own coordinator, which the spec allows.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: handoff (M9)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule BringRequested {
    when: PageBrings(origin, destination)
    let s = if origin.intent = following then origin.source else origin
    ensures:
        HandoffRun.created(kind: bring, origin: origin, destination: destination, source: s, started: now)
        CoordinatorReceives(destination, follow, s.name)
        when PlayerAvailableAt(s, destination):
            CoordinatorReceives(origin, silence)

    @guidance
        -- The page sends the last two requests only after the source's
        -- status lists the destination in its audience, so the music is
        -- playing in the destination before it stops in the origin. A
        -- pinned focus stays pinned; if the pinned room goes silent it is
        -- no longer a member and ApplyFocus falls back to the default
        -- until it listens again. If that does not happen within
        -- config.player_ready_timeout the origin keeps playing and the
        -- page says why.
}

rule MoveRequested {
    when: CoordinatorReceives(destination, move_from, source)
    let s = Room{name: source}
    ensures:
        if s.status.library_id != destination.library_id:
            Reply(refused, library_differs)
        else if s.status.intent = following:
            Reply(refused, source_is_following)
        else:
            let session = s.hand_over(destination.name)
            LoadSession(destination, session)
            destination.intent = own
            destination.source = null
            for f in session.audience:
                SendspinServerHolds(destination, f)
            destination.focus = session.focus
            destination.focus_pinned = session.focus_pinned
            ApplyFocus(destination)
            Reply(done)
            HandoffRun.created(kind: move, origin: s, destination: destination, source: s, started: now)

    @guidance
        -- hand_over on the old source reads its queue, current song,
        -- elapsed time, play state, modes, audience and focus, pauses
        -- its MPD,
        -- sets moved_to = destination, and returns the session. The
        -- destination loads the queue in one command list, seeks to the
        -- elapsed time plus the time the hand-over took, and plays, then
        -- its server holds every player of the old audience, which
        -- displaces the old source by Sendspin's multiple-server rule.
        -- Each follower adopts the destination as its source on its next
        -- reconcile, from moved_to. The old source returns to own and
        -- paused through AudienceGoneForGood, and clears moved_to then.
        -- Gap budget: config.move_gap_budget.
}

rule LoadSession {
    when: LoadSession(room, session)
    ensures:
        room.mpd_playing = session.state = playing

    @guidance
        -- command_list_begin: clear, add each URI, repeat, random,
        -- single, consume, seek <current> <elapsed>, play if playing;
        -- command_list_end. URIs are portable between rooms because every
        -- room mounts the same export at the same music_directory, which
        -- libraryId checks.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: the shared library and playlists (M12, M13)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule WriterPublishesDatabase {
    when: ScanCompletes(room)
    requires: room.library_writer
    ensures:
        PublishedDatabase.created(writer: room, path: room.library_published, version: now, library_id: room.library_id)

    @guidance
        -- After nixtune.allium's ScanCompletes: copy MPD's database to
        -- <published>.tmp beside the published file, with the export
        -- stamp, then rename over it. A rename within one directory on
        -- one NFS server is atomic, so a reader never copies half a
        -- database.
}

rule ReaderAdoptsDatabase {
    when: every config.database_poll for room: Room where not library_writer
    let p = PublishedDatabase.latest
    requires: p.version > room.database_version
    requires: p.library_id = room.library_id
    requires: not room.mpd_playing
    requires: (room.audience + room.suspended).count = 0 and room.intent = own and room.path = direct
    ensures:
        room.database_version = p.version
        MpdRestarts(room)

    @guidance
        -- MPD loads its database only at start and has no reload
        -- command, so adoption is copy then restart, and waits until the
        -- room is idle. restore_paused brings the queue back. A reader
        -- never runs `update`: its rescan timer is off and the page's
        -- rescan control is hidden (capabilities libraryWriter false).
        -- The art cache stays per room.
}

-- Saved playlists need no rule of their own: every room's MPD has
-- playlist_directory = multiroom.playlists.path on the NAS. The page
-- lists playlists again whenever the Saved pane opens, because another
-- room's save raises no idle event here.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: the page
------------------------------------------------------------

rule PageDrawsRooms {
    when: RoomsPanelOpened(client)
    ensures:
        for p in client.served_by.peers:
            PageSubscribes(p, status)

    @guidance
        -- One websocket per peer coordinator while the panel is open;
        -- each pushes a status line whenever RoomStatusChanged fires, so
        -- the panel redraws from pushes, the way the page redraws from
        -- MPD's idle events. A peer that does not answer is drawn as
        -- unreachable, never guessed.
}

rule PageControlsSource {
    when: TransportPressed(client, command)
    let r = client.served_by
    let target = if r.intent = following then r.source else r
    ensures: MpdReceives(target, command)

    @guidance
        -- The page opens an MPD websocket to the source's /ws and shows
        -- that queue while its room is following. Library browsing stays
        -- on its own room's MPD (the database is the same everywhere,
        -- M12) and adds go to the source by URI.
}

rule PageSetsGroupVolume {
    when: GroupVolumeMoved(client, step)
    let g = DerivedGroup(client.served_by)
    ensures:
        for m in g.members:
            VolumeHelperReceives(m, step)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant OneThingOnTheDac {
    -- Extends nixtune.allium's SingleOutputOwner.
    for r in Rooms:
        (if r.alsa_output_enabled and r.mpd_playing then 1 else 0)
        + (if r.path in {own_server, peer_server} then 1 else 0)
        + (if r.path = interrupted then 1 else 0) <= 1
}

invariant AloneIsBitPerfect {
    -- M4, allowing one audience_grace for the switch.
    for r in Rooms:
        (r.intent = own and (r.audience + r.suspended).count = 0 and r.audience_empty_since = null)
            implies r.path in {direct, interrupted}
}

invariant SourceRoomHearsItsOwnServer {
    for r in Rooms:
        (r.intent = own and (r.audience + r.suspended).count > 0) implies r.path in {own_server, interrupted}
}

invariant OneGroupPerSpeaker {
    -- Sendspin: a player holds at most one admitted playback connection.
    for r in Rooms:
        Count(s in Rooms where r in s.audience or (s = r and r.held_by = r)) <= 1
}

invariant InterruptionsAlwaysEnd {
    -- Every room interrupted is either still interrupted by a running
    -- source, or has been restored.
    for r in Rooms where path = interrupted:
        InterrupterActive(r, r.interrupted_by)
}

invariant FocusRoomWaitsLongest {
    -- M14: within a group, the focus room's added acoustic delay is the
    -- largest, and every added delay is non-negative.
    for g in Groups:
        for m in g.members:
            AddedDelay(g.focus) >= AddedDelay(m) and AddedDelay(m) >= 0
}

invariant DeclaredOnly {
    -- M7, M15. Every Sendspin connection that reaches playback is
    -- between a declared server key and a declared player key, keyed by
    -- a provisioned PSK.
    for c in SendspinConnections where playback:
        c.server_key in DeclaredServerKeys and c.player_key in DeclaredPlayerKeys and c.psk_category = long_term
}

invariant VolumeIsPerRoom {
    -- M6.
    for r in Rooms:
        OutputDevice{host: r}.written_by in {helper, airplay}
}

invariant NoStoredGroups {
    -- M2.
    not Exists(GroupRecord)
}

invariant OneLibraryWriter {
    Count(r in Rooms where library_writer) = 1
}

invariant MultiroomOffChangesNothing {
    for r in Rooms where not multiroom.enable:
        Closure(r) = Closure(r with multiroom removed)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Host {
    identified_by: Client where role = host
}

actor PeerRoom {
    identified_by: Room
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- On the host page, beside Queue and Library. Guests do not group
-- rooms; a guest page in a following room queues to the source.
surface RoomsPanel {
    facing host: Host

    exposes:
        for p in host.served_by.peers:
            p.status.intent
            p.status.source
            p.status.audience
            p.status.suspended
            p.status.path
            p.status.interrupted_by
            p.status.focus
            p.reachable

    provides:
        Follow(room: host.served_by, target: Room)
        Leave(room: host.served_by)
        Bring(origin: Room, destination: host.served_by)
        Move(source: Room, destination: host.served_by)
        Silence(room: Room)
        PinFocus(room: Room)
        UnpinFocus()
        GroupVolume(step: Integer)
        Calibrate(focus: host.served_by, room: Room, travel: Duration)

    @guidance
        -- One row per room, followers drawn under their source so groups
        -- read as lists, the focus room marked. "Play here" on another
        -- room's row is Bring; "Take over" is Move. Two-press confirm for
        -- Move and Silence, as nixtune-page.allium does for deletes. An
        -- interrupted room shows what interrupted it and what it will
        -- return to. Calibration is a separate pane: pick the other
        -- room, move the slider until the clicks merge, read off the
        -- number to declare.
}

surface RoomProtocol {
    facing peer: PeerRoom

    provides:
        status()
        follow(target: String)
        own()
        silence()
        hold(room: String)
        suspend(room: String)
        move_from(source: String)
        hand_over(to: String)
        focus(room: String, pinned: Boolean)
        calibrate(focus: String, room: String, travel: Duration)

    contracts:
        Coordinator
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Publishing (M17)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- packages.<sys>.nixtune-room for x86_64-linux and aarch64-linux, both
-- static musl binaries built by haskell.nix, the aarch64 one cross-built
-- on x86_64 so no builder needs an ARM machine. checks.<sys> gains
-- nixtune-room's test suite and MultiroomRehearsal. CI pushes both
-- packages to the binary cache named in the flake's nixConfig. The
-- README says nixtune speaks the Sendspin protocol, in plain text,
-- which the Open Home Foundation's trademark terms allow without
-- permission; no part of nixtune is named after Sendspin.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Verification
------------------------------------------------------------

-- MultiroomRehearsal: a NixOS VM test with three nodes (kitchen, lab,
-- living-room), each with the snd-dummy kernel module as its card, MPD playing
-- a generated tone, nixtune-room, and the pinned, patched sendspin-cli
-- player, with test keys generated by `nixtune-room keys` at build time.
-- No audio is listened to; the test asserts on the coordinators' status
-- lines, the units each node runs, MPD's outputs and the delays each
-- player reports. Planned cases:
--   follow: the source switches to own_server, the follower to
--     peer_server; leave: after audience_grace both are direct again;
--   a source follows a third room: its own audience stays with it, its
--     speaker goes to the third room;
--   pause in a group: every player is cleared and MPD's elapsed moves
--     back by the unheard audio;
--   focus: with declared acoustics, each player's output_delay matches
--     M14 for each focus; pinning moves it and nothing else does;
--   the source's VM stops: followers keep their intent, it starts again,
--     they are held again; stopped past orphan_timeout, they return to
--     own and paused;
--   bring: the origin goes silent only after the destination is in the
--     audience;
--   move: the session's URIs, position and modes arrive intact, the old
--     audience is held by the destination, and moved_to clears;
--   interruption in a follower (a stub calling interruption_starts):
--     it is suspended, the source keeps its path, and when it ends the
--     room is held again;
--   interruption in a source room: the group plays on; when it ends
--     the room hears its own server again;
--   interruption in a lone room: MPD pauses and resumes as today;
--   a test foreign server with a declared key interrupts and the room
--     returns; one without a declared key is refused in the handshake;
--   a player whose provisioned key does not match its declaration does
--     not start;
--   the writer publishes, a reader adopts only while idle;
--   a room with multiroom off builds the same closure as today.
-- Runs under `nix flake check` beside checks.rehearsal. A conformance
-- run of nixtune-room against aiosendspin's reference client and against
-- sendspin-rs's player example belongs here too, so the server is
-- checked against implementations nixtune did not write. Sync accuracy
-- and acoustic alignment are not measurable in a VM; they are measured
-- on the real rooms, by calibration.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Deferred
------------------------------------------------------------

deferred Sendspin.core               -- see: github.com/Sendspin/spec README.md, connection.md, messaging.md
deferred Sendspin.pairing            -- see: github.com/Sendspin/spec pairing.md
deferred Sendspin.player_v1          -- see: github.com/Sendspin/spec roles/player/v1.md
deferred Sendspin.metadata_v1        -- see: github.com/Sendspin/spec roles/metadata/v1.md
deferred Sendspin.controller_v1      -- remotes and Home Assistant devices driving a group
deferred Sendspin.visualizer_v1      -- the party and lights direction
deferred Multiroom.native_player     -- a player that replaces sendspin-cli behind SendspinPlayer
deferred Multiroom.speaker_only      -- Sendspin devices with no MPD (ESPHome, browsers) as group members
deferred Multiroom.native_rate       -- per-track formats, which needs MPD to signal format changes
deferred Multiroom.mic_calibration   -- acoustics measured by a microphone instead of by ear
deferred Multiroom.focus_follows_listener -- focus moved by page touch or presence instead of pinning
deferred Sendspin.code_pairing       -- CPace pairing for devices without provisioned keys
deferred Multiroom.renderer_to_group -- AirPlay or Bluetooth audio played to a whole group
deferred Nixtune.player              -- see: nixtune.allium
deferred Nixtune.page                -- see: nixtune-page.allium

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "1. The group stream format: 44100:24:2 (drafted) or 48000:24:2."
open question "2. Whether a MOVE should first BRING the destination into the group and only then transfer, so the gap falls in a moment of the user's choosing."
open question "3. Whether the NAS gets a second share for the published database and playlists, or they live as folders inside the music export."
open question "4. Whether a room's intent should survive its own reboot (M8 drafts no)."
open question "5. When the microphone calibration comes (deferred Multiroom.mic_calibration): a USB microphone on one room, or a phone's microphone through the page, which needs the page served over HTTPS because browsers allow getUserMedia only in a secure context."
open question "6. Whether nixtune-room stays on nixpkgs' Haskell set (stage 4) or moves to M17's static musl binary from haskell.nix. nixpkgs already carries every dependency for aarch64-linux in cache.nixos.org, so a Pi builds only nixtune-room itself, in minutes, and the attic can hold that one result; haskell.nix adds an input, a second GHC and its own cache, and pays off only for adopters off NixOS or without a builder."
